[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1811","stub":{"id":"1811","section":"3. Farm Road 3351 to Rebecca Creek Crossing","altname":"","river":"Guadalupe","difficulty":"II","states":["USA-TEX"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"62","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Public access is at river left (north of the bridge) on the downstream side via a dirt/gravel road that runs along the highway easement.  Paddlers can also pay to park and put in at Bergheim Campground.  The campground also rents canoes and kayaks and runs shuttles for an additional fee.","id":"104701","location":{"latitude":"29.892200469970703","longitude":"-98.55899810791016"},"name":"FM 3351 Crossing (Bergheim)","distance":"0","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"portage","description":"This low-water crossing can become clogged wtih debris, and at least one person has drowned by becoming trapped in debris under the crossing.  There is no real parking here.","id":"104704","location":{"latitude":"29.88599967956543","longitude":"-98.53199768066406"},"name":"Edge Falls Road (Low-Water Crossing)","distance":"3.61","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"Public access at river right with a paved parking lot and bathroom facilities.  Camping is also available in the state park (do not camp along the river.)  Day use fee or State Parks Pass required to put in/take out.\n\nHistorical footnote: David Bamberger, former CEO of Church's Fried Chicken, owned the primary tract of land that became Guadalupe River State Park.  In the early 70s, he agreed to sell the land to the state at a price significantly below market value, because he recognized the need for public river access along the Upper Guadalupe.  Bamberger is also a conservation pioneer, and his ranch (Selah Bamberger Ranch) at the headwaters of Miller Creek serves as a model for both wildlife and groundwater (spring) conservation in the Texas Hill Country.","id":"104705","location":{"latitude":"29.875999450683594","longitude":"-98.48580169677734"},"name":"Guadalupe River State Park","distance":"7.75","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"hazard","description":"Known to be runnable at higher flows, but portage if it looks the least bit sketchy.","id":"104706","location":{"latitude":"29.881000518798828","longitude":"-98.47660064697266"},"name":"Low-Water Crossing","distance":"10.25","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"Public access and parking is available at river left (at the end of Old Spring Branch Rd.)  This is a popular put-in for doing a longer day trip down to Rebecca Creek Rd.  The relatively flat stretch of river from Nichol's Landing to U.S. 281 is also popular with tubers during the summer.\n\nSouthwest Paddler warns that vehicles parked here after dark will be towed, but it is unknown whether that beta is current.","id":"104709","location":{"latitude":"29.8799991607666","longitude":"-98.447998046875"},"name":"Nichol's Landing","distance":"12.65","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"No public access, but you can pay one of the river outfitters.","id":"104710","location":{"latitude":"29.853700637817383","longitude":"-98.40789794921875"},"name":"U.S. 281 Bridge","distance":"16.42","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"Not a very good access point.  Requires parking along the highway and hiking up/down a fairly steep hill to get to the river.","id":"104712","location":{"latitude":"29.860700607299805","longitude":"-98.38369750976562"},"name":"FM 311 Bridge","distance":"18.03","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"takeout","description":"A new high bridge was completed in 2013, with a paved takeout area at river left on the upstream side.  You can temporarily park your vehicle here while taking out, but do not leave it here.  Instead, park it in the gravel along the downriver side of Rebecca Creek Rd. about 150 yards up the hill from the takeout.","id":"104716","location":{"latitude":"29.88800048828125","longitude":"-98.36830139160156"},"name":"Rebecca Creek Crossing","distance":"22.51","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"10469","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8427","description":"The best rapid we experienced that day, Rock Pile Rapid is located 1 mile downstream from our put in, FM3351. I had the pleasure of borrowing the photographer’s kayak, a Dagger Blackwater 10.5 (skeg up!) for this shot. The half-day 8 mile adventure took us to the take out, Guadalupe State Park.","caption":"Let's Ride!","poi_name":"Rock Pile Rapid","subject":"\"Cactus Carl\"","author":"N. F.","poi_id":"104714","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10469","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10469"}},{"id":"885337","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888055","description":"","caption":"Travertine Falls","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885337","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885337"}},{"id":"885338","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888056","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885338","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885338"}},{"id":"10152","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7595","description":"","caption":"Scouting the Dog Leg","poi_name":"Dog Leg","subject":"","author":"M Driver","poi_id":"104703","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10152","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10152"}},{"id":"9832","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7596","description":"Rock Pile Rapid is about 1 mile below FM 3351","caption":"Upper Guad in the Spring","poi_name":"Rock Pile Rapid","subject":"","author":"M Driver","poi_id":"104702","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9832","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9832"}},{"id":"885336","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888054","description":"","caption":"Rockpile Rapid","poi_name":"Rockpile Rapid","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":"104702","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885336","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885336"}}],"detail":{"id":"1811","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Upper Guadalupe is a relatively beginner-friendly stretch of river popular with canoeists and recreational kayakers looking to paddle in a more secluded setting, free of the wall-to-wall tubers and private homes that adorn the Lower Guadalupe.  Except in runoff events, during which the river can be very silty, it is usually fed by spring flow and thus runs clean.  Except during extreme droughts, the Upper Guadalupe generally maintains a reasonable base flow.\n\nThe Upper Guad does not have a lot of gradient, so there are only a handful of named rapids on this entire 25-mile stretch of river.  Rapids are typically short and are separated by pools sometimes miles long.  Thus, whitewater boaters tend to avoid the Upper Guad unless it is flowing thousands of cfs, and they tend to prefer the stretch below U.S. 281, because it has the largest rapids (including Mueller Falls and Rust Falls) and the least flat water.\n\nWith the exception of the afore-mentioned falls, most of the rapids are basic Class I-II rock gardens with little consequence.\n\nThe most popular day trips are from Bergheim (FM 3351) to Guadalupe River State Park (7.8 miles) and from Nichol's Landing to Rebecca Creek Rd. (9.8 miles, often shortened to 6 miles by putting in at U.S. 281.)  The latter stretch has been designated a [Texas Paddling Trail](http://tpwd.texas.gov/fishboat/boat/paddlingtrails/) by the Texas Parks & Wildlife Dept.\n\nFor more information, see [Texas Whitewater, 2nd Edition](http://philosophy.tamu.edu/~sdaniel/kayak.html).\n\nDistances and gradient measured using GIS tools in 2015.","averageGradient":"6","maxGradient":null,"length":"22.6","correlations":[{"reachID":"1811","isPrimary":false,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"200","beginMediumRunnable":"1000","beginHighRunnable":"5000","endHighRunnable":"10000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"Guadalupe Rv at Comfort, TX","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"08167000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"47","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:50:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.87","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:50:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"29.96523889","longitude":"-98.8971667"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/08167000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/08167000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"47","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:50:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}},{"reachID":"1811","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeC